WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.320
How do you use the

2
00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.720
regular pumping lemma correctly? We

3
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.460
will prove that equal blocks

4
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.020
of zeros followed by ones

5
00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.820
cannot be recognized by any

6
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.300
finite automaton. The crucial issue

7
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.680
is who chooses the split,

8
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.220
not whether we can find

9
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.920
one convenient split to break.

10
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.980
If a language is regular,

11
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.040
some pumping length p works

12
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.000
for every sufficiently long word

13
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.700
in it. Each such word

14
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.720
has some decomposition x y

15
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.480
z with y nonempty and

16
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.840
x y of length at

17
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.300
most p. Every repetition count,

18
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.620
including zero, must keep that

19
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.080
chosen decomposition inside the language.

20
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.940
The repeated-state proof explains these

21
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.160
conditions. The first p symbols

22
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.520
of an accepted run visit

23
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.040
p plus one state positions,

24
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.660
forcing a repeated state among

25
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.160
p states. The nonempty intervening

26
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.140
segment is a loop that

27
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.460
can be removed or repeated

28
00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.080
without changing the final destination.

29
00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.900
Assume equal-block language regular and

30
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.440
let p be its alleged

31
00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.740
pumping length. Choose the word

32
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.480
with p zeros followed by

33
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.340
p ones, which belongs to

34
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:04.620
the language and has length

35
00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.620
at least p. We choose

36
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.340
this witness, but the lemma

37
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.860
gets to supply the decomposition.

38
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.820
Take any legal decomposition of

39
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.280
that witness. Because the first

40
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.840
p positions are zeros and

41
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.460
x y ends within them,

42
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.700
y consists of k zeros

43
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.180
for some k between one

44
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.680
and p. This covers every

45
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.020
permitted decomposition, not just the

46
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.460
split where y happens to

47
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.540
be the whole first block.

48
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.080
Now choose zero repetitions. Removing

49
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.220
y leaves p minus k

50
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.520
zeros followed by p ones,

51
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.200
so the two block counts

52
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.400
are different. Since k is

53
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.260
positive, the pumped word is

54
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:42.880
outside the language for every

55
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.220
legal split, contradicting the lemma.

56
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.820
For a picture, use four

57
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.660
zeros and four ones, with

58
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.240
a loop piece containing two

59
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.640
zeros. Removing that piece leaves

60
00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.220
two zeros and four ones,

61
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.340
visibly breaking equality. That illustration

62
00:01:58.340 --> 00:01:59.840
helps you see the mechanism,

63
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:01.280
but the proof is the

64
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.040
arbitrary-k argument, not this single

65
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.000
numerical split.

66
00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.640
A common wrong proof declares

67
00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.040
x empty and y equal

68
00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.000
to all p zeros. It

69
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.800
correctly breaks that one split,

70
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.920
but the lemma only promised

71
00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.120
some successful split and could

72
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.200
have chosen another. A true

73
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.080
nonregularity conclusion does not rescue

74
00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.400
that quantifier mistake.

75
00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.860
The lemma is necessary for

76
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.180
regularity, not sufficient. Some nonregular

77
00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.660
languages still satisfy its pumping

78
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.740
conclusion, so failure to find

79
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.680
a contradiction does not establish

80
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.600
regularity. For positive regularity, construct

81
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.640
a finite automaton or expression;

82
00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.220
for another negative tool, use

83
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.640
distinguishable prefixes.

84
00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:47.440
Things to take note of.

85
00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.860
State the lemma correctly, verify

86
00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.940
witness membership and length, and

87
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.320
describe every legal decomposition before

88
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.600
choosing a repetition count. End

89
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.700
with explicit failed membership and

90
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.020
contradiction, and remember that zero

91
00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.140
repetitions are allowed even though

92
00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.940
the repeated piece itself must

93
00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:05.460
be nonempty.
